        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Sets</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these dumbbells. They were chosen from manufacturer product and warranty
            pages, Amazon listings, recall records and independent coverage, and the claims on this
            page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We looked for fixed dumbbell sets sold with, or alongside, a rack, that were listed and in
            stock on Amazon US on 19 October 2026 and on the maker's own store, and that we could
            cross-check: Garage Gym Reviews has reviewed REP's rubber hex dumbbells and CAP's 150 lb set
            with rack, and for the other sets we compared the maker's listing with the Amazon listing.
            We covered both common coatings, rubber and urethane, and a range of sizes, because the
            right set depends more on the room and the budget than on the brand.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The recall check, and the Amazon Basics recall</h3>
          <p>
            Every brand on this page — CAP Barbell and REP Fitness — and every brand we considered and
            left out was searched in the CPSC's recall database on 19 October 2026, by name in recall
            titles and with the word dumbbell. We found no recall of any fixed dumbbell or dumbbell set
            from any brand on this page. REP's one recall record, from 2021, concerns strap safety
            brackets for racks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every dumbbell recall we found in recent years involves an adjustable design. The one most
            relevant to Amazon shoppers is Amazon's May 2026 recall of its <strong>Amazon Basics 55 Lbs.
            Adjustable Dumbbells</strong>: the notice says the plates can fail to engage completely
            during weight adjustment and dislodge during use, covers about 2,900 units sold on Amazon
            from July to September 2025, and identifies them by the lot code C250306VJAW5 on the outer
            box. It does not cover Amazon Basics' fixed rubber hex dumbbells, which were on sale on 19
            October as single dumbbells. Our
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/#check-recall" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbell recall checklist</a>
            lists that recall alongside the BowFlex, FitRx, ProForm, NordicTrack and PowerBlock
            notices. A fixed dumbbell has no mechanism to fail in that way, which is one of the
            quiet arguments for buying one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we left out, and why</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Rogue.</strong> Rogue's rubber hex and urethane dumbbells are well regarded —
              Garage Gym Reviews names Rogue's urethane dumbbells its best dumbbell set — but Rogue sells
              only through its own store, not on Amazon, so they are outside this list.</li>
            <li><strong>Titan Fitness.</strong> On 19 October Titan's store listed round urethane sets
              (5 to 50 lb at $2,024.99) and stainless hex dumbbells, but no rubber hex set, and we could
              not confirm a Titan dumbbell set on Amazon.</li>
            <li><strong>Yes4All 150 lb Rubber Hex Set with Rack.</strong> A popular Amazon set, but its
              listing showed "currently unavailable" on 19 October.</li>
            <li><strong>Amazon Basics.</strong> Amazon Basics sold rubber hex dumbbells as singles on
              19 October, but we found no Amazon Basics set with a rack. Singles are a reasonable way to
              build a small set; see <a href="#price-per-pound" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">price per pound</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Cast-iron hex sets.</strong> CAP sells a 550 lb cast-iron hex set with rack for
              about the same price as its rubber set. We preferred rubber for home use: Garage Gym
              Reviews found the enamel on CAP's cast-iron hex dumbbells chipped easily, and bare iron is
              harder on floors and louder.</li>
            <li><strong>Neoprene and vinyl sets.</strong> Light sets with a stand, mostly under $100,
              suit toning and rehab-style work but are outside the scope of a strength set.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

